  "blog": [
   {
    "title": "the sticker misprint (a confession)",
    "mood": "guilty (not really)",
    "body": "a girl came into the store tuesday with six dollars in coins and held up a record that cost fourteen. you know the look. she'd counted it in the car. i said, 'oh, that's a misprint on the sticker, it's six.' it wasn't. i have since misprinted thirty-one stickers. i am a menace to my own inventory. the woman who runs the good record store noticed in april and said, 'vinyl_witch, our margins,' and i said, 'the stars were in a mood,' and she said 'okay' and walked away. she has never mentioned it again. a record doesn't belong to the one who can afford it, darling. it belongs to the one who will play it start to finish with the lights off. the girl came back in august. she said it was the first thing she'd ever owned that she didn't have to share. i went in the back and pretended to organize the 7 inches. i'm fine. there's dust back there."
   },
   {
    "title": "a household spell for the skipping record (in five steps)",
    "mood": "practical",
    "body": "since the question comes up weekly: your record skips. it is almost never the record. step one: clean it, with a soft brush, moving with the grooves, as if petting a cat that has opinions. step two: look at the needle under a bright light. if it's furry, you're not a witch, you're a vacuum. step three: leveling. is the table on a wobbly shelf? it can feel your fear. step four: some people put a coin on the headshell, but only if they know what they're doing, which is why i won't tell you which coin. step five: say something kind to it. i'm serious. i once watched a record stop skipping after a customer said 'sorry, buddy' to it. i won't explain that. i kept a straight face and sold him a sleeve. that's the whole spell. the rest is owning a good table and a little faith."
   },
   {
    "title": "limewire_larry sent me a mixtape (a review)",
    "mood": "bewildered, pleasantly",
    "body": "the CD is labeled NICK DRAKE - GREATEST HITS in silver marker. there are eleven tracks. track 1 is nick drake. i want to be very clear: it is a low-quality copy, and it is the most beautiful low-quality copy i have ever heard, because there's a faint chirp in the left channel like a cricket having a private feeling. track 2 is a different band entirely. track 3 is forty seconds of a man describing a tractor. track 4 is the same man, describing a different tractor. track 5 is a slow song i can't identify, and it made me sit down. i've played it nine times. its title is 'untitled_final_v2.' what i've learned from larry: mislabeling is a form of surprise. i have asked him for track 5's real name. he doesn't know. we are going to find out. together."
   },
   {
    "title": "rules for tuesday listening night (posted by the door, posted here for the nervous)",
    "mood": "ceremonial",
    "body": "1. four chairs. a fifth person sits on the floor. the floor is also a chair. 2. one side, start to finish. no skipping. no explaining. 3. phones off, or at least face down, as a sign of respect, like a hat. 4. you may close your eyes. you may not fall asleep, but i'll forgive it once. 5. when the needle lifts, nobody speaks for the length of one breath. 6. tea is provided. it's always the same tea. don't ask what's in it (tea). 7. you may cry. there are tissues under the third chair. there are always tissues under the third chair. last week an older man came in with his late wife's copy of a record he'd never listened to, because it was 'hers.' we played side A. five of us. nobody said anything for four breaths. it's the longest it's ever been. that's the whole reason i do this."
   }
  ]
