  "blog": [
   {
    "title": "regional recap: 3 hours there, 3 hours back, 7 games in between",
    "mood": "stiff",
    "body": "left sacramento at 6:10 am. dad drove, i held the saltines. 3 hours to fresno, 2 gas station stops, 1 gas station burrito i regret less than the match. the venue was freezing, they always are, so i used all 4 hand warmers: one per pocket, two for the thumbs. i packed the whole setup, yes including the CRT, i'm not an animal. won my first set 3-0. won the second 3-1. then winners semis and i drew dale, the marth main from stockton who teaches middle school band. he beat me 3-1 and said 'good game, sorry!' after every stock. he MEANS it. that's the worst part. i went to losers and lost 3-2 in a last-hit game i will think about in the shower until november. final place: 17th. dad said 'that's more than most.' i said 'it's out of 40.' he said 'then it's still more than most.' he's the only coach i've ever needed. we got home at 1 am. mom left a plate on the counter with a note: 'positive!' and a smiley face."
   },
   {
    "title": "balloon arches: how i pay for 20XX",
    "mood": "light-headed",
    "body": "people ask how i afford a hobby where every event costs $20 to enter, plus gas, plus the gas station burrito. the answer is balloons. i work tuesdays, thursdays and saturdays at a party supply place called Fiesta Barn. i blow up balloons. i have built 31 arches. one arch is one bracket entry. i think in arches now. a regional is two arches. a major is a whole wedding. i can tie a balloon dog in 11 seconds and my thumbs have never been stronger. here is the secret nobody tells you: balloon work is basically frame data. you have to know exactly how much pressure before it pops. too much and you're done. too little and the whole arch droops. i learned my release timing from a balloon fox. the ears were wrong. they are always wrong. a kid at a birthday party said it looked 'okay.' that's a nine star review."
   },
   {
    "title": "my cousin maya beat me (a statement, i'm fine)",
    "mood": "calm (visibly)",
    "body": "my cousin maya is nine and mains jigglypuff. i taught her everything: the buttons, how to shield, how to not mash. she picked jigglypuff because 'she's round.' sunday: friendlies. maya beat me 2 out of 5. i'd like to make three points. one, i was going easy. two, i wasn't. three, after the third match she said 'you're thinking too much, wes,' and my whole life flashed. she's right. i overthink every bracket set. maya doesn't think. she pushes the stick and hums. the lesson: in melee the kid with zero fear is a lot of fox. i'm trying to lose gracefully. today i shook her hand and said 'gg.' then i went to the garage and said 'gg' to the wall nine times at different volumes. on her suggestion i've taken up humming while i play. it's going badly. it's going well? she says it's going well. she's nine and she runs my whole life."
   },
   {
    "title": "the rules of friday friendlies (posted at the bottom of the basement stairs)",
    "mood": "organized",
    "body": "1. ports get inspected. i won't be sorry. 2. the winner doesn't pick the stage. the loser does. it's called mercy. 3. no johns. a john is any excuse. 'my controller drifted' is a john. 'the sun was in my eyes' in a basement is a double john. 4. you can say gg or you can say nothing. you cannot say 'that was lucky.' 5. my mom brings out orange slices at halftime. you eat one. it is not optional, she's watching from the stairs. 6. any kid under 12 gets first pick of character. i don't care if it's jigglypuff. 7. the last set of the night is always 2v2, where we all lose together. 8. nobody leaves until we've cleaned the controller ports as a group. rubbing alcohol and cotton swabs. it's very bonding. last week 11 people came, 4 of them new. one of them moved samus like water. i have never been so afraid or so happy."
   }
  ]
